# WordPress Plugin Plan Rule

When writing a Plan for this repository, treat this as a custom WordPress Gutenberg block plugin.

Before proposing code changes, review the relevant official WordPress documentation for the type of change being planned. Prefer official WordPress developer documentation over blog posts or general JavaScript/React advice.

Use these documentation sources as standing references:

- WordPress Plugin Developer Handbook: https://developer.wordpress.org/plugins/
- WordPress Plugin Security / Common APIs Security Handbook: https://developer.wordpress.org/apis/security/
- WordPress Coding Standards Handbook: https://developer.wordpress.org/coding-standards/
- WordPress PHP Coding Standards: https://developer.wordpress.org/coding-standards/wordpress-coding-standards/php/
- WordPress Block Editor Handbook: https://developer.wordpress.org/block-editor/
- Block development fundamentals: https://developer.wordpress.org/block-editor/getting-started/fundamentals/
- Block file structure: https://developer.wordpress.org/block-editor/getting-started/fundamentals/file-structure-of-a-block/
- block.json fundamentals: https://developer.wordpress.org/block-editor/getting-started/fundamentals/block-json/
- Block metadata reference: https://developer.wordpress.org/block-editor/reference-guides/block-api/block-metadata/
- Block registration reference: https://developer.wordpress.org/block-editor/reference-guides/block-api/block-registration/

Every Plan must include a short “WordPress best-practice check” section that confirms whether the proposed change affects any of the following:

- block.json metadata or attributes
- edit.tsx editor behavior
- save.tsx saved markup
- view.ts frontend behavior
- style.scss frontend CSS
- editor.scss editor-only CSS
- PHP registration or plugin bootstrap code
- accessibility: keyboard, focus, ARIA, screen reader labels
- security: escaping, sanitization, nonces, permissions, direct file access
- internationalization: __(), _x(), sprintf(), text domain usage
- backward compatibility or block validation risk
- build output in /build

For this plugin, follow these project conventions:

- Prefer child-owned attributes when a setting belongs to a child block.
- Use parent attributes only for settings that control the overall parent block or are shared by multiple children.
- Use block context only when a parent-owned value must be consumed by descendant blocks.
- Keep editor-only instructional text outside visual preview containers when it would distort the preview.
- Avoid hardcoded IDs in reusable block markup. Use BEM-style classes for styling and JavaScript selectors.
- Do not change saved markup lightly. If saved markup changes, call out possible Gutenberg block validation impact.
- Prefer small, branch-sized changes with a clear test plan.
- Do not implement code until the Plan has been reviewed and accepted.

When writing a Plan, include:

1. Goal
2. Files likely affected
3. WordPress best-practice check
4. Proposed implementation steps
5. Risks / validation concerns
6. Manual test checklist
7. Suggested commit message

Example best-practice check:

```markdown
## WordPress best-practice check
- block.json: yes — new child attribute `padding`
- edit.tsx / save.tsx: yes — inspector control + saved class
- view.ts: no
- style.scss / editor.scss: yes / no
- PHP: no
- a11y / security / i18n: labels via `__()`; no new user input
- validation /build: saved markup class change — existing blocks may need deprecation
```
